  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why are horizontal and vertical motion independent?",
      "a": "Gravity acts only vertically, so it changes the vertical velocity but not the horizontal one. Without air resistance, the sideways speed stays constant throughout the flight.",
      "asks": [
        "why does horizontal velocity stay constant"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I calculate the range of a projectile?",
      "a": "On level ground, the range is u squared times sin of two theta, divided by g. Or find the flight time from the vertical motion and multiply it by the horizontal speed.",
      "asks": [
        "range formula projectile"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does 45 degrees give the longest range?",
      "a": "The range depends on sin of two theta, which is largest, equal to one, when two theta is 90 degrees, so theta is 45 degrees. This holds on level ground without air resistance.",
      "asks": [
        "best angle for maximum range"
      ]
    },
